<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leucon I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 389–349 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leucon I was ruler of the Bosporan Kingdom from 389 to 349 BCE, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartocid-dynasty">Spartocid dynasty</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Theodosia</a> and extended his rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindi">Sindi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeotians">Maeotians</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a>, turning a union of Greek cities into a territorial state. He granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> tax-free purchase of grain and was honoured with Athenian citizenship, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> records.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leucon, also known as Leuco, was born around 410 BCE or earlier, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satyrus-i">Satyrus I</a> and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartocus-i">Spartocus I</a>, a man of Thracian descent who had usurped the Greek Archaeanactid dynasty. He may have been taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a>. He was present at his father's unsuccessful war against Tirgatao, queen of the Ixomatae, in which his brother Metrodoros died as a hostage. When Satyrus died during the first siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Theodosia</a>, Leucon became king jointly with his brother Gorgippus, who ruled the Asiatic side from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgippia">Gorgippia</a>, a city he named after himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Leucon made peace with the Ixomatae and turned west. A first siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Theodosia</a> failed when Tynnichus, a general sent by Heraclea Pontica, relieved the city by trickery; five years later Leucon took it by surprise and then beat a Heracleote attack on his own territory, placing his Scythian soldiers in the rear with orders to strike down any of his men who fled. He then intervened in a dynastic dispute of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindi">Sindi</a>, defeated Octamasades at the Battle of Labrytai and was proclaimed king of all the Sindike. An inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphaion">Nymphaion</a> calls him archon of the Bosporus, Theodosia and all Sindike.</p><p>To his Greek subjects he was archon, to the tribes king, and the Athenians saw him as a tyrant but a friend. He put down a rebellion by members of his court with the help of merchants, and recalled the region's coins to reissue them at double their face value. He gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> 400,000 medimnoi of grain free of charge, and statues of him and his sons were set up there. Late in his reign Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, probably hired by Heraclea Pontica, defeated a Bosporan army by a ruse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leucon died in 349 BCE after a reign of around forty years, and his body is thought to have been placed in the Royal Kurgan on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a>. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Theodosia</a>, possibly a daughter of the Bosporan diplomat Sopaeus, had borne him three sons, Spartocus II, Apollonius and Paerisades I, and Spartocus II and Paerisades I succeeded him. His descendants ruled the Bosporus for another two centuries, until Paerisades V died during a Scythian uprising.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leucon I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Satyrus I (433–389 BCE) and <b>Leucon I</b> (389–349 BCE) turned the union of Greek cities into a territorial state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Leucon I</b> of the Bosporan Kingdom captured Theodosia, probably c. 355 BCE, and developed its harbour for the grain trade with Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/theodosia">Theodosia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in the Bosporan Kingdom. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.34,36.47&amp;year=-369" aria-label="Panticapaeum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5416 564 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leucon I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5866 834h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5866 834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.34,36.47&amp;year=-369">What was here in 369 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
